 **Cloud-obscured days (optional, `PYRO_CLOUD_FILE`).** A satellite can pass over and see nothing because cloud hides the ground; FIRMS files carry no cloud information, so an observed day with 0 detections used to be read as "no fire". Give `harmonize` a CSV with one row per UTC day (columns `date` and `clear_fraction`, the share of your area of interest that was clear, 0-1; `cloud_fraction`, `clear_percent` and `cloud_percent` also work; an optional `satellite` column restricts a row to one satellite) and an observed day with **0 detections and a clear share below `PYRO_CLOUD_MIN_CLEAR` (default 0.5)** becomes `harmonized_status = observed_obscured`: harmonized value `NaN`, never scored by `anomaly`. A day that is not in the file has unknown cloud cover and keeps its old status; it is never treated as clear. Days with detections keep their status and are only flagged (`viirs_cloud_limited`); `PYRO_CLOUD_OBSCURE_NONZERO=1` also marks them `observed_obscured`. The calendar gains `viirs_clear_fraction` and `viirs_cloud_limited`, and `frp_mapping.json` gains a `cloud_obscuration` block (days covered, days marked, warnings). Without `PYRO_CLOUD_FILE` nothing changes. Build the file by averaging, per UTC day, the clear share of a cloud-mask product (e.g. MOD35 / MYD35, or a VIIRS cloud mask) clipped to the same area as your fire data (`cloud_mask.clear_share_from_mod35` decodes the MOD35 `Cloud_Mask` byte); check it first with `python -m src.cloud_mask your_file.csv`. Details: `docs/METHODOLOGY.md` section 2.5.
 
+**Why the harmonized series is VIIRS-referenced, and where MODIS enters.** The harmonized record is expressed on the VIIRS 375 m scale on purpose. A merged MODIS+VIIRS series needs one sensor's value to stand in for the other on days one is missing, and the data do not support that here: on the shipped Egypt data VIIRS totals are ~9x the k-scaled Aqua totals and correlate weakly with them (`daily_total_relationship`), because k is a ratio conditional on a MODIS detection and cannot recover the many small fires only VIIRS sees. MODIS therefore contributes four things, none of them a silent substitution: (1) the footprint-matched pixel table (`matched_pairs.csv`); (2) the per-satellite scale k with its CI, prediction interval and trust grade, i.e. the quantitative bridge between the two records; (3) MODIS expressed on the VIIRS scale with uncertainty (`modis_frp_viirs_equiv_mw`, `_lo_mw`, `_hi_mw`), so the pre-VIIRS / MODIS-only record can be read on the same axis; (4) `modis_only_evidence` on days VIIRS did not observe. Terra has no k against an afternoon VIIRS reference (~150 min apart) and so adds only detection counts; `PYRO_APPLY_UNVALIDATED_K=1` applies an unvalidated k and labels every value.
+
+**Time-gap window of k (J1 / NOAA-20).** `PYRO_K_MAX_DT_MIN` defaults to `auto`: matches within 30 minutes are used, and only if no MODIS satellite has enough of them the window is widened to 45, 60, 75 or 90 minutes (`PYRO_K_WIDEN_STEPS`). Aqua vs NOAA-20 is ~50 minutes apart, so with the J1 file you will see `k window: ... widened to 60 min`. A widened k is capped below HIGH trust and recorded in `frp_mapping.json` (`scale_policy.k_window`); part of its ratio is fire evolution between the overpasses. Set a number to fix the window.
+
+**Single-day demo (FIRMS 24h files).** The global 24h NRT files never cover a full UTC day, so by default their one day is `partial_day` with no harmonized value. For a demo run
+
+```bash
+PYRO_SINGLE_DAY_DEMO=1 python -m src.harmonize
+```
+
+The partial day becomes `demo_partial_window` with the VIIRS total (a lower bound) and a printed summary of VIIRS vs MODIS-on-the-VIIRS-scale totals and k. It is a pipeline demonstration, never a validated record, and `anomaly` ignores it.
+
 **Spatial grid.** Matching, k and every daily FRP total use native pixels and do not depend on the 0.5 degree grid; the grid is used for the bootstrap clusters, the active-cell counts, the cell-level correlations and `calendar_cells.csv`. `grid_sensitivity.csv` re-computes those quantities for several cell sizes; see `docs/METHODOLOGY.md`, section 4, for why 0.5 degrees, what it loses, and how to read the cell metrics (they are properties of the grid; quote them with `cell_deg`).
 
 Limits: 24h NRT files only demonstrate the pipeline. A usable calendar needs years of archive data.
